1
00:01:34,469 --> 00:01:37,681
Thank you so much.
That looks fabulous.

2
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:39,558
Thank you, Geoffrey.

3
00:01:39,558 --> 00:01:40,601
You're welcome.

4
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:43,979
We'll also take a bottle of red
as well. Thank you.

5
00:01:43,979 --> 00:01:45,314
Certainly.

6
00:01:45,314 --> 00:01:48,400
Right, now...

7
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,152
Thank you, Barry.
You're welcome.

8
00:01:50,152 --> 00:01:51,361
Thank you.

9
00:01:51,361 --> 00:01:54,573
That looks wonderful.
Beautiful day.

10
00:01:55,657 --> 00:01:57,159
Where are you going?

11
00:01:57,159 --> 00:01:58,285
To eat my sandwich.

12
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:00,203
You'll ruin your appetite for prawns.

13
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:01,538
I don't like prawns.

14
00:02:01,538 --> 00:02:03,040
Darling, don't wander off.

15
00:02:03,915 --> 00:02:06,627
For God's sake,
leave her alone, Deirdre.

16
00:02:06,627 --> 00:02:08,420
Go play with your ball.

17
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:09,796
Don't be so annoying!

18
00:02:15,552 --> 00:02:18,930
Darling, what about snakes?

19
00:02:20,057 --> 00:02:22,643
There aren't any snakes on the beach.

20
00:02:22,643 --> 00:02:24,519
Just let her enjoy herself for once.

21
00:02:27,898 --> 00:02:29,399
Edward?
Yes, ma'am.

22
00:03:03,642 --> 00:03:05,435
Hello, there.

23
00:03:05,435 --> 00:03:09,147
What are you,
a little bird or something?

24
00:03:13,985 --> 00:03:18,323
Are you hungry?
Take a bite. It's roast beef.

25
00:03:18,323 --> 00:03:21,118
It's good. Come on.
I won't hurt you.

26
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:27,332
Mommy! Daddy! You've got to
come see this! I found something!

27
00:03:41,054 --> 00:03:43,432
Cathy, darling, lunch is ready!

28
00:03:45,016 --> 00:03:47,060
Are you looking at this?

29
00:03:47,728 --> 00:03:50,522
I'm afraid there isn't enough
to go around.

30
00:03:56,111 --> 00:03:57,279
Paul?

31
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:00,574
Cathy?

32
00:04:03,535 --> 00:04:06,413
Will you hurry up?
Hurry up!

33
00:05:04,095 --> 00:05:05,972
You're him, right?

34
00:05:07,432 --> 00:05:09,309
Excuse me?

35
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:12,270
The scientist? The guy?
I saw you on TV.

36
00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:16,775
I believed you.

37
00:05:48,890 --> 00:05:51,393
Who shall I tell Mr. Hammond
is calling?

38
00:05:51,393 --> 00:05:54,229
Ian Malcolm.
I've been summoned.

39
00:06:16,585 --> 00:06:18,461
Dr. Malcolm!

40
00:06:18,461 --> 00:06:19,754
Oh, my God.

41
00:06:20,714 --> 00:06:22,549
Hello, Dr. Malcolm.

42
00:06:22,549 --> 00:06:24,175
Kids, kids.

43
00:06:24,175 --> 00:06:27,679
It's so great to see you.

44
00:06:27,679 --> 00:06:30,098
It's so great.
Look at you.

45
00:06:30,098 --> 00:06:31,725
You came to see Grandpa?

46
00:06:31,725 --> 00:06:34,978
Yeah, he called me.
Do you know what it's about?

47
00:06:36,229 --> 00:06:40,275
I don't, either. This joint's
kind of creepy, isn't it?

48
00:06:40,275 --> 00:06:41,985
Is everything okay?

49
00:06:41,985 --> 00:06:43,737
Well, not exactly.

50
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:57,667
Dr. Malcolm.

51
00:06:57,667 --> 00:07:01,838
Dr. Malcolm. Here to share a few
campfire stories with my uncle?

52
00:07:07,844 --> 00:07:10,931
You can convince The Washington Post
and Skeptical Inquirer

53
00:07:10,931 --> 00:07:12,098
of whatever you want.

54
00:07:12,098 --> 00:07:14,809
But I was there. I know
what happened and so do you.

55
00:07:14,809 --> 00:07:18,396
Do you actually believe that
everyone who chose discretion did so

56
00:07:18,396 --> 00:07:21,650
for a nefarious motive?
Even Lex and Tim?

57
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:24,486
Leave them out of it.
It's not a game.

58
00:07:24,486 --> 00:07:27,447
No. You signed
a nondisclosure agreement

59
00:07:27,447 --> 00:07:28,698
before you went to the island

60
00:07:28,698 --> 00:07:31,701
that forbade you from
discussing what you saw.

61
00:07:31,701 --> 00:07:32,869
You violated that agreement.

62
00:07:32,869 --> 00:07:36,331
I did. And you lied about
the deaths of three people.

63
00:07:36,331 --> 00:07:39,501
You misinformed the public,
which made me look like a nut.

64
00:07:39,501 --> 00:07:41,419
Hasn't been so good
for my livelihood...

65
00:07:41,419 --> 00:07:44,381
We made a generous compensatory offer
for your injuries.

66
00:07:44,381 --> 00:07:45,966
A payoff and an insult.

67
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:48,426
When you spin reality,
cover up evidence,

68
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:50,804
it hurts more than my reputation.

69
00:07:50,804 --> 00:07:52,472
As I recall,

70
00:07:53,765 --> 00:07:56,101
your university revoked your tenure

71
00:07:56,101 --> 00:07:57,894
for your selling wild stories
to the press.

72
00:07:57,894 --> 00:08:01,189
I didn't sell anything. I never
took a cent, and I told the truth.

73
00:08:01,189 --> 00:08:02,565
Your version of it.

74
00:08:02,565 --> 00:08:05,110
There aren't any "versions"
of the truth.

75
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:06,611
InGen can't keep spewing out...

76
00:08:06,611 --> 00:08:09,197
InGen is my responsibility now.

77
00:08:09,197 --> 00:08:11,199
I will jealously defend
its interests.

78
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:13,326
Your responsibility?
What about Mr. Hammond?

79
00:08:15,036 --> 00:08:18,832
It is our board of directors
I must face, not my uncle.

80
00:08:18,832 --> 00:08:22,627
Trust me, your problems are
about to be rendered moot.

81
00:08:22,627 --> 00:08:26,339
In a few weeks' time,
they'll be long forgotten.

82
00:08:28,425 --> 00:08:30,093
Not by me.

83
00:08:30,093 --> 00:08:31,386
Careful.

84
00:08:33,847 --> 00:08:36,725
This suit cost more
than your education.

85
00:08:38,518 --> 00:08:40,979
You were right and I was wrong.
There!

86
00:08:40,979 --> 00:08:44,274
Did you ever expect
to hear me say such a thing?

87
00:08:45,483 --> 00:08:47,318
Thank God for Site B.

88
00:08:48,903 --> 00:08:50,321
Site B?

89
00:08:51,156 --> 00:08:53,408
Isla Nublar was just a showroom,

90
00:08:53,408 --> 00:08:55,201
something for the tourists.

91
00:08:55,201 --> 00:08:56,828
Site B was the factory floor.

92
00:08:56,828 --> 00:09:00,040
That was on Isla Sorna,
80 miles from Nublar.

93
00:09:00,582 --> 00:09:04,210
We bred the animals there,
and nurtured them for a few months,

94
00:09:04,210 --> 00:09:06,755
then moved them into the park.

95
00:09:06,755 --> 00:09:08,840
Really? I did not know that.

96
00:09:09,674 --> 00:09:11,885
After the accident in the park,

97
00:09:11,885 --> 00:09:15,680
Hurricane Clarissa wiped out
our facility on Site B.

98
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:17,557
Call it an act of God.

99
00:09:18,683 --> 00:09:21,811
We had to evacuate.
The animals were released

100
00:09:21,811 --> 00:09:24,022
to mature on their own.

101
00:09:24,022 --> 00:09:28,568
Life will find a way,
as you once so eloquently put it.

102
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:31,905
By now we have a complete
ecological system on the island

103
00:09:31,905 --> 00:09:35,033
with dozens of species
living in their own social groups

104
00:09:35,033 --> 00:09:39,204
without fences, boundaries,
or constraining technology.

105
00:09:39,204 --> 00:09:41,039
And for four years

106
00:09:41,790 --> 00:09:45,460
I've tried to keep it safe
from human interference.

107
00:09:46,377 --> 00:09:48,713
That's right. Hopefully,
you've kept the island

108
00:09:48,713 --> 00:09:51,257
quarantined and contained.

109
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:53,384
But I'm in shock about all this.

110
00:09:53,384 --> 00:09:55,637
I mean, that they're still alive.

111
00:09:55,637 --> 00:09:57,889
You bred them lysine-deficient.

112
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:00,058
Shouldn't they have kicked
after seven days

113
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:01,768
without supplemental enzymes?

114
00:10:01,768 --> 00:10:04,395
Yes! But, by God,
they're flourishing.

115
00:10:04,395 --> 00:10:07,649
That's one of 1,000 questions
I want the team to answer.

116
00:10:07,649 --> 00:10:09,192
Team?

117
00:10:09,192 --> 00:10:10,193
Yes.

118
00:10:11,986 --> 00:10:14,989
I've organized an expedition to go in,

119
00:10:14,989 --> 00:10:17,742
and, thank you,
and document them.

120
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:20,995
To make the most
spectacular living fossil record

121
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:22,288
the world has ever seen.

122
00:10:22,288 --> 00:10:25,834
Go in and document?
You mean with people?

123
00:10:25,834 --> 00:10:29,420
Yes, the animals won't even know
they're there. Very low impact.

124
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:32,715
Strictly observation
and documentation.

125
00:10:32,715 --> 00:10:37,762
Our satellite infrareds show that
the animals are fiercely territorial.

126
00:10:37,762 --> 00:10:40,932
The carnivores are isolated
in the interior of the island

127
00:10:40,932 --> 00:10:44,394
so the team can stay
on the outer rim.

128
00:10:44,394 --> 00:10:47,522
Don't worry. I'm not making
the same mistakes again.

129
00:10:47,522 --> 00:10:50,525
No, you're making all new ones.

130
00:10:51,526 --> 00:10:53,027
John!

131
00:10:53,027 --> 00:10:55,655
Okay. So, there's another island
with dinosaurs,

132
00:10:55,655 --> 00:10:59,492
no fences this time,
and you want to send people in,

133
00:10:59,492 --> 00:11:03,496
very few people,
on the ground, right?

134
00:11:03,496 --> 00:11:06,541
Who are these four lunatics
that you're trying to con into this?

135
00:11:06,541 --> 00:11:09,502
It was difficult to convince them
as to what they would see,

136
00:11:09,502 --> 00:11:12,130
in the end had to use my checkbook
to get them there.

137
00:11:12,130 --> 00:11:14,799
But there's Nick Van Owen,

138
00:11:14,799 --> 00:11:17,051
who is a video documentarian.

139
00:11:18,386 --> 00:11:22,182
Eddie Carr,
who's a field equipment expert.

140
00:11:23,224 --> 00:11:26,144
We have our paleontologist

141
00:11:26,144 --> 00:11:30,607
and I was hoping that perhaps
you might be the fourth.

142
00:11:31,858 --> 00:11:35,737
We've been on the verge of Chapter 11
ever since that accident in the park.

143
00:11:35,737 --> 00:11:39,574
There are those in the company
who wanted to exploit Site B

144
00:11:39,574 --> 00:11:41,576
in order to bail us out.

145
00:11:41,576 --> 00:11:43,703
They've been planning it for years,

146
00:11:43,703 --> 00:11:46,372
and I've been able
to stop them until now.

147
00:11:46,372 --> 00:11:50,919
But, a few weeks ago,
a British family on a yacht cruise

148
00:11:50,919 --> 00:11:56,799
stumbled across the island
and their wee girl was injured.

149
00:11:56,799 --> 00:11:58,468
She's fine. She's fine.

150
00:11:58,468 --> 00:12:02,597
But the board has used the incident

151
00:12:02,597 --> 00:12:05,016
to take control of InGen from me.

152
00:12:05,016 --> 00:12:08,353
Now it's only a matter
of time before this

153
00:12:08,978 --> 00:12:12,148
lost world is found and pillaged.

154
00:12:13,483 --> 00:12:16,527
Public opinion is the one thing
I can use to preserve it,

155
00:12:16,527 --> 00:12:18,863
but to rally that support

156
00:12:18,863 --> 00:12:21,532
I need a complete photo record

157
00:12:21,532 --> 00:12:25,078
of those animals alive
and in their natural habitat.

158
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:28,414
So you went from capitalist
to naturalist in just four years.

159
00:12:29,374 --> 00:12:30,458
That's something.

160
00:12:33,419 --> 00:12:37,257
It's our last chance at redemption.

161
00:12:40,927 --> 00:12:42,220
John,

162
00:12:43,263 --> 00:12:44,472
no.

163
00:12:45,139 --> 00:12:47,392
Of course, no.

164
00:12:47,392 --> 00:12:51,271
And I'm going to contact the other
three members of your team

165
00:12:51,271 --> 00:12:53,398
and stop them from going.

166
00:12:53,398 --> 00:12:56,067
Who's the paleontologist, by the way?

167
00:12:56,067 --> 00:12:58,403
She came to me,
I want you to know this.

168
00:12:59,487 --> 00:13:00,613
Who did?

169
00:13:00,613 --> 00:13:04,117
Leave it to you, Ian,
to have associations, affiliations,

170
00:13:04,117 --> 00:13:07,453
even liaisons, with the best people
in so many fields.

171
00:13:07,453 --> 00:13:08,830
Not Sarah.

172
00:13:08,830 --> 00:13:11,833
Paleontological behavior study
is a new field,

173
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:13,876
and Sarah Harding
is on that frontier.

174
00:13:13,876 --> 00:13:15,712
Her theories on
parenting and nurturing

175
00:13:15,712 --> 00:13:18,423
amongst carnivores
have framed the debate.

176
00:13:18,423 --> 00:13:19,590
What are you doing?

177
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:21,175
Where's your phone?

178
00:13:24,345 --> 00:13:25,930
It's too late.

179
00:13:26,681 --> 00:13:28,516
She's already there.

180
00:13:33,604 --> 00:13:35,857
The others are meeting her
in three days.

181
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:44,449
You sent my girlfriend
to this island alone?

182
00:13:45,116 --> 00:13:48,328
Sent is hardly the word.
She couldn't be restrained.

183
00:13:48,328 --> 00:13:50,330
She was already working in San Diego,

184
00:13:50,330 --> 00:13:52,457
doing some research
at the animal park.

185
00:13:52,457 --> 00:13:55,043
It's only a couple of hours flight
from there.

186
00:13:55,043 --> 00:13:58,838
She was adamant, absolutely
adamant about, here you are,

187
00:13:58,838 --> 00:14:02,133
about making the initial foray
by herself.

188
00:14:02,133 --> 00:14:04,218
She thinks she's Dian Fossey.

189
00:14:04,218 --> 00:14:06,387
Observation without interference,
she said.

190
00:14:06,387 --> 00:14:08,806
She went on and on.
You know how it is.

191
00:14:08,806 --> 00:14:12,810
After you were injured in the park,
she sought you out.

192
00:14:12,810 --> 00:14:16,022
Then she went all the way
to a hospital in Costa Rica

193
00:14:16,022 --> 00:14:19,817
to ask somebody who she didn't even know
if the rumors were true.

194
00:14:19,817 --> 00:14:22,779
If you want to leave your name
on something, fine.

195
00:14:22,779 --> 00:14:26,491
But stop putting it on
other people's headstones.

196
00:14:26,491 --> 00:14:31,204
She'll be fine. She's spent
years studying African predators.

197
00:14:31,204 --> 00:14:35,166
Sleeping downwind and all.
She knows what she's doing.

198
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:37,335
And, believe me,
the research team will...

199
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:41,047
It's not a research expedition
anymore.

200
00:14:41,047 --> 00:14:44,384
It's a rescue operation
and it's leaving now.

201
00:15:04,237 --> 00:15:06,197
You can't shave three days
off my deadline

202
00:15:06,197 --> 00:15:07,490
and expect to be ready.

203
00:15:07,490 --> 00:15:09,909
I'm not fully supplied.
I haven't tested any of this.

204
00:15:09,909 --> 00:15:11,536
Why give her a satellite phone

205
00:15:11,536 --> 00:15:13,246
if it doesn't work?

206
00:15:13,246 --> 00:15:16,499
Could be solar flares, the satellite,
or maybe she turned it off.

207
00:15:16,499 --> 00:15:20,420
I need half air on the tires.
We're flying pressurized.

208
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:21,712
Does she know how to use it?

209
00:15:21,712 --> 00:15:22,839
You kidding?

210
00:15:22,839 --> 00:15:25,758
She's faxed me refinements on
50% of the plans for this stuff.

211
00:15:25,758 --> 00:15:28,886
Don't do that. You got to baby
it a little bit. Love it.

212
00:15:28,886 --> 00:15:30,096
I'll love it when it works.

213
00:15:30,096 --> 00:15:31,931
It'll work when you love it.

214
00:15:31,931 --> 00:15:33,015
You coming along?

215
00:15:33,015 --> 00:15:35,518
I don't usually, but I can't resist.

216
00:15:35,518 --> 00:15:37,520
Thanks for the two-minute warning.

217
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,397
Nick Van Owen, this is Ian Malcolm.

218
00:15:39,397 --> 00:15:40,898
Nick's our field photographer.

219
00:15:40,898 --> 00:15:42,692
How do you do?

220
00:15:42,692 --> 00:15:44,277
You do wildlife photography?

221
00:15:44,277 --> 00:15:46,362
Wildlife, combat, you name it.

222
00:15:46,362 --> 00:15:50,074
When I was with Nightline,
I was in Rwanda, Chechnya, Bosnia.

223
00:15:50,074 --> 00:15:52,910
I do volunteer work with Greenpeace
once in a while.

224
00:15:52,910 --> 00:15:55,371
Greenpeace?
What drew you there?

225
00:15:55,371 --> 00:15:56,747
Women.

226
00:15:57,415 --> 00:15:59,083
80% female, Greenpeace.

227
00:15:59,750 --> 00:16:00,918
Noble.

228
00:16:01,752 --> 00:16:04,797
Noble was last year.
This year I'm getting paid.

229
00:16:04,797 --> 00:16:09,093
Hammond's check cleared, or I wouldn't
go on this wild-goose chase.

230
00:16:09,093 --> 00:16:12,889
You're going to the only place in the world
where the geese chase you.

231
00:16:13,806 --> 00:16:15,224
Dad! Dad!

232
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:20,980
Kelly, my honey, you found it.
What took you so long?

233
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:22,607
Sorry, I couldn't get a cab.

234
00:16:22,607 --> 00:16:23,983
Sweetie, that's okay.

235
00:16:23,983 --> 00:16:27,904
Listen, I've got to tell you something.
I've got to talk to you.

236
00:16:28,905 --> 00:16:30,865
I don't even know this woman.

237
00:16:30,865 --> 00:16:33,159
You've known Karen for 10 years.

238
00:16:33,159 --> 00:16:36,621
She doesn't even have Sega.
She's such a troglodyte.

239
00:16:36,621 --> 00:16:39,123
Cruel, but good word use.

240
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:41,167
Why can't I stay with Sarah?

241
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:44,295
She's out of town.
Karen's fantastic.

242
00:16:44,295 --> 00:16:47,298
She'll take you places.
You'll have a fantastic time.

243
00:16:47,298 --> 00:16:50,426
Stop saying "fantastic".
Where are you going, anyway?

244
00:16:51,093 --> 00:16:55,515
It's only for a few days. I wouldn't
be going if it wasn't really important.

245
00:16:55,515 --> 00:16:56,974
I'm your daughter all the time.

246
00:16:56,974 --> 00:17:00,520
You can't abandon me
whenever opportunity knocks.

247
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:03,314
That hurts my feelings.
Your mom tell you to say that?

248
00:17:03,314 --> 00:17:06,275
Dr. Malcolm, downstairs, please.

249
00:17:06,275 --> 00:17:08,528
I know we've had some hard going.

250
00:17:08,528 --> 00:17:12,323
But in the last few years,
we've started to work things out.

251
00:17:12,323 --> 00:17:14,825
Yeah, but crack on me a bit.

252
00:17:14,825 --> 00:17:16,786
Ground me or something.
Send me to my room.

253
00:17:16,786 --> 00:17:18,829
You never do any of that stuff.

254
00:17:18,829 --> 00:17:21,165
Why would I?
You turned out so

255
00:17:21,165 --> 00:17:24,835
beautiful, brilliant, powerful,
funny, and generous.

256
00:17:24,835 --> 00:17:27,004
The queen, goddess.
My inspiration.

257
00:17:27,004 --> 00:17:28,297
Dr. Malcolm.

258
00:17:28,297 --> 00:17:32,051
I could come be your research assistant,
like I was in Austin.

259
00:17:32,051 --> 00:17:34,345
This is nothing like Austin.

260
00:17:35,846 --> 00:17:38,182
Anyway, you have your own stuff.

261
00:17:38,182 --> 00:17:41,519
Your gymnastics competition.
You've been training for months.

262
00:17:41,519 --> 00:17:43,187
Gymnastics?

263
00:17:43,187 --> 00:17:45,231
I scrubbed out, Dad,
I got cut from the team.

264
00:17:45,231 --> 00:17:46,941
Thanks for knowing.

265
00:17:47,775 --> 00:17:49,902
I'm sorry, honey.

266
00:17:50,861 --> 00:17:53,239
I know how much that meant to you.

267
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,618
You like to have kids, but you just
don't want to be with them, do you?

268
00:18:00,162 --> 00:18:02,498
Hey, I'm not the one
who dumped you here

269
00:18:02,498 --> 00:18:05,209
and split for Paris,
so don't take it out on me.

270
00:18:05,209 --> 00:18:07,545
Dr. Malcolm, downstairs!

271
00:18:13,884 --> 00:18:16,137
Honey, I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

272
00:18:17,805 --> 00:18:21,267
You want some good parental advice?
Don't listen to me.

273
00:18:24,437 --> 00:18:26,230
How are we doing here?

274
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:32,069
The specs say it can't deform at
12,000 PSI, so we just have to test it.

275
00:18:32,069 --> 00:18:33,404
Let's clear. Are we clear?

276
00:18:33,404 --> 00:18:34,614
What's this?

277
00:18:36,324 --> 00:18:37,742
A High Hide.

278
00:18:38,451 --> 00:18:41,954
A High Hide. You know,
you go up and hide, high.

279
00:18:41,954 --> 00:18:44,081
It goes up to where the trees are

280
00:18:44,081 --> 00:18:46,917
and keeps the researchers
out of harm's way.

281
00:18:47,585 --> 00:18:50,713
Actually, it puts them at
a very convenient biting height.

282
00:18:51,839 --> 00:18:53,591
What's the time?
Do you have the time?

283
00:18:53,591 --> 00:18:55,217
Do I have the time? Why?

284
00:18:55,217 --> 00:18:56,761
We're leaving in three hours.

285
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:17,948
This is so cool.

286
00:20:17,091 --> 00:20:21,178
Lindstradt air rifle fires a subsonic
Fluger impact delivery dart.

287
00:20:21,178 --> 00:20:22,722
It works better than
your satellite phone?

288
00:20:22,722 --> 00:20:24,223
That's funny.

289
00:20:24,223 --> 00:20:26,976
I loaded it with the enhanced venom
of Conus Purpurascens,

290
00:20:26,976 --> 00:20:28,352
South Sea cone shell.

291
00:20:28,352 --> 00:20:30,438
Most powerful neurotoxin
in the world.

292
00:20:30,438 --> 00:20:31,856
Acts within 0.002 of a second.

293
00:20:31,856 --> 00:20:34,316
Which is faster than
the nerve-conduction velocity.

294
00:20:34,316 --> 00:20:37,653
So the animal's down before
it even feels the prick of the dart.

295
00:20:37,653 --> 00:20:38,988
Is there an antidote?

296
00:20:38,988 --> 00:20:40,990
Like if you shot yourself
in the foot?

297
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:42,116
Don't do that.

298
00:20:42,116 --> 00:20:44,869
You'd be dead before you even realized
you had an accident.

299
00:20:49,248 --> 00:20:51,542
The guy says that he wants to unload
at the shore, right here.

300
00:20:51,542 --> 00:20:53,294
He won't go any further up the river.

301
00:20:53,294 --> 00:20:55,880
He's heard too many stories
about this island chain,

302
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:57,506
and he wants to drop us off

303
00:20:57,506 --> 00:20:59,800
and go anchor somewhere offshore.
What kind of stories?

304
00:21:05,473 --> 00:21:07,975
He's heard stories of fishermen
that came too close to the island

305
00:21:07,975 --> 00:21:10,060
and never returned.

306
00:21:10,853 --> 00:21:14,064
He has a radio.
He has a satellite phone.

307
00:21:16,609 --> 00:21:20,237
When you need him, send a call
and he'll be here in two hours.

308
00:21:20,237 --> 00:21:22,072
But he will not stay here.

309
00:21:23,574 --> 00:21:25,534
He won't stay anywhere
near these islands.

310
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:28,829
They call the islands...

311
00:21:28,829 --> 00:21:30,790
"Las Cinco Muertes"?

312
00:21:32,041 --> 00:21:33,209
What does that mean?

313
00:21:35,211 --> 00:21:36,754
The Five Deaths,

314
00:21:37,296 --> 00:21:38,714
he says.

315
00:21:53,354 --> 00:21:56,482
I put a location sensor
into Dr. Harding's satellite phone,

316
00:21:56,482 --> 00:21:57,817
so we should get a reading.

317
00:21:57,817 --> 00:21:59,443
Yeah, I'm so relieved.

318
00:21:59,443 --> 00:22:01,278
Don't push my buttons.

319
00:22:01,278 --> 00:22:04,907
We're almost on top of her dot.
She should be around here.

320
00:22:05,616 --> 00:22:06,992
Over there.

321
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:22,132
Sarah!

322
00:22:24,635 --> 00:22:26,929
Sarah Harding!

323
00:22:26,929 --> 00:22:29,807
How many Sarahs do you think
are on this island?

324
00:22:40,985 --> 00:22:42,152
What?

325
00:22:44,321 --> 00:22:46,198
Something big.

326
00:23:16,312 --> 00:23:19,690
This is magnificent.

327
00:23:19,690 --> 00:23:22,735
Yeah...
That's how it always starts.

328
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:26,405
But then later there's
running and screaming.

329
00:23:51,430 --> 00:23:52,598
Nick!

330
00:23:55,726 --> 00:23:58,437
I guess you got the jump on us,
didn't you, Sarah?

331
00:23:58,437 --> 00:23:59,146
Yeah.

332
00:24:04,068 --> 00:24:05,945
Ian, I never thought
in a million years

333
00:24:05,945 --> 00:24:08,113
Hammond could get you to come here.

334
00:24:10,574 --> 00:24:13,077
Hi, Eddie.
Hi, Sarah.

335
00:24:13,077 --> 00:24:16,413
Got a granola bar or something?
I'm starving.

336
00:24:18,290 --> 00:24:20,209
Did you see those animals?

337
00:24:20,209 --> 00:24:22,670
A family group,
a pair-bond and a sub-adult,

338
00:24:22,670 --> 00:24:24,630
long after the juvenile
was nest bound.

339
00:24:24,630 --> 00:24:27,758
Every egg clutch I've seen
has crushed, trampled shells.

340
00:24:27,758 --> 00:24:30,260
Hatchlings stay in the birth environment
a long time.

341
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:31,470
That's conclusive.

342
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:35,265
I can put that controversy to rest
if I get a shot of the nest.

343
00:24:35,265 --> 00:24:37,267
Have you been attacked?

344
00:24:37,267 --> 00:24:40,771
No, that's my lucky pack.
That's how it always looks.

345
00:24:42,982 --> 00:24:44,441
Okay, Sarah...

346
00:24:44,441 --> 00:24:47,277
Is that a Nikon?
Can I borrow that?

347
00:24:48,070 --> 00:24:50,280
I'll be right back, baby, I promise.

348
00:24:53,283 --> 00:24:56,787
When Hammond called you,

349
00:24:56,787 --> 00:24:58,414
why didn't you say something to me?

350
00:24:58,414 --> 00:25:00,791
I knew you would have
stopped me from coming.

351
00:25:00,791 --> 00:25:03,168
I would have tied you to the bed.

352
00:25:03,168 --> 00:25:05,629
I know how they survived
without lysine.

353
00:25:05,629 --> 00:25:06,755
I don't care.

354
00:25:06,755 --> 00:25:09,508
The herbivore species
that are thriving

355
00:25:09,508 --> 00:25:13,220
eat mostly agama beans,
soy, anything lysine-rich.

356
00:25:13,220 --> 00:25:16,348
And the carnivores eat
the herbivores, so...

357
00:25:18,350 --> 00:25:19,810
Wait.

358
00:25:21,437 --> 00:25:23,772
Stay there. Be quiet.
I'll be right back.

359
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:24,982
No!

360
00:25:27,693 --> 00:25:29,319
Stay there.

361
00:25:59,850 --> 00:26:01,185
She's much too close.

362
00:26:01,185 --> 00:26:02,770
What the hell is she doing?

363
00:26:02,770 --> 00:26:04,396
Too close.

364
00:26:25,292 --> 00:26:28,921
She has to touch it.
She can't not touch.

365
00:26:30,172 --> 00:26:32,091
She's going right up to it.

366
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:33,842
Once she sees something,
she has to...

367
00:26:42,893 --> 00:26:44,853
Is this even possible?

368
00:26:46,063 --> 00:26:47,106
What?

369
00:26:48,774 --> 00:26:51,068
This? What did you
think you'd document?

370
00:26:51,068 --> 00:26:52,736
What did you think you'd see?

371
00:26:52,736 --> 00:26:55,072
Animals, maybe,

372
00:26:57,074 --> 00:26:58,617
big iguanas.

373
00:27:01,578 --> 00:27:02,996
Fruitcakes.

374
00:27:11,004 --> 00:27:13,590
They get very angry
when you run out of film.

375
00:27:14,341 --> 00:27:16,260
Sarah!

376
00:27:24,059 --> 00:27:25,310
Shoot them!

377
00:27:25,310 --> 00:27:26,812
They're just protecting their baby.

378
00:27:26,812 --> 00:27:28,814
So am I!

379
00:27:49,918 --> 00:27:51,753
I think they're leaving.

380
00:28:00,971 --> 00:28:03,140
These images are incredible.
Legendary.

381
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:06,310
Guys shoot their whole life,
they never get stuff half this good.

382
00:28:06,310 --> 00:28:09,354
Just give me the Pulitzer right now.
Today, please.

383
00:28:09,354 --> 00:28:11,732
The competition's over,
close the entries,

384
00:28:11,732 --> 00:28:13,650
I'd like to thank everyone who lost.

385
00:28:13,650 --> 00:28:17,321
Don't light that! Dinosaurs pick
up scents from miles away.

386
00:28:17,321 --> 00:28:20,199
We're here to observe
and document, not interact.

387
00:28:20,199 --> 00:28:23,118
Which, by the way,
is a scientific impossibility.

388
00:28:23,118 --> 00:28:24,620
Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle.

389
00:28:24,620 --> 00:28:26,663
What you study, you also change.

390
00:28:26,663 --> 00:28:30,042
I'll risk it. I'm sick of scratching
around in rock and bone,

391
00:28:30,042 --> 00:28:33,170
and making assumptions
and deductions about

392
00:28:33,170 --> 00:28:36,340
the nurturing habits of animals that
have been dead for 65 million years.

393
00:28:36,340 --> 00:28:37,507
I'm sick of it, man.

394
00:28:37,507 --> 00:28:39,718
Then you show up and fill my head
with stories for four years,

395
00:28:39,718 --> 00:28:41,345
so of course I'm going to
come down here.

396
00:28:41,345 --> 00:28:44,139
Stories of mutilation and death.
Weren't you paying attention?

397
00:28:44,139 --> 00:28:46,975
Please! Don't treat me like
I'm a grad student.

398
00:28:46,975 --> 00:28:49,811
I've worked around predators
since I was 20 years old.

399
00:28:49,811 --> 00:28:52,356
Lions, jackals, hyenas, you.

400
00:29:00,405 --> 00:29:02,532
Where's the fire?

401
00:29:02,532 --> 00:29:05,452
I'm trying to change 100 years
of entrenched dogma.

402
00:29:05,452 --> 00:29:09,289
Dinosaurs were characterized
very early on as vicious lizards.

403
00:29:09,289 --> 00:29:12,793
There's great resistance to the idea
of them as nurturing parents.

404
00:29:12,793 --> 00:29:15,379
Robert Burke said
the T-Rex was a rogue

405
00:29:15,379 --> 00:29:17,839
who abandoned its young
at the earliest opportunity.

406
00:29:17,839 --> 00:29:19,466
I know I can prove otherwise.

407
00:29:19,466 --> 00:29:22,844
Fire! Dr. Malcolm!
Fire, base camp!

408
00:29:28,058 --> 00:29:31,520
No! Water makes the smoke billow.
Use dirt!

409
00:29:31,520 --> 00:29:33,480
Who started the fire?

410
00:29:34,815 --> 00:29:37,025
I just wanted to make dinner.

411
00:29:37,818 --> 00:29:40,570
I wanted it ready
when you guys got back.

412
00:29:42,990 --> 00:29:45,867
Do you see any family
resemblance there?

413
00:29:46,576 --> 00:29:48,912
You practically told me to come here.

414
00:29:48,912 --> 00:29:50,747
I... what?

415
00:29:50,747 --> 00:29:52,749
You said, don't listen to me.

416
00:29:52,749 --> 00:29:54,751
I thought you were trying
to tell me something.

417
00:29:54,751 --> 00:29:57,587
Kelly, you knew exactly what I meant.
You have no...

418
00:29:57,587 --> 00:29:59,548
You want to lock her up
for her curiosity?

419
00:29:59,548 --> 00:30:01,425
Where do you think she gets it?

420
00:30:01,425 --> 00:30:02,759
Thank you, Sarah.

421
00:30:02,759 --> 00:30:06,138
Don't start the teaming-up thing
about this.

422
00:30:07,347 --> 00:30:10,517
Out of the conversation.
Please.

423
00:30:10,517 --> 00:30:12,978
Really. Eddie?
What?

424
00:30:12,978 --> 00:30:15,439
Why in the hell
doesn't this thing ever work?

425
00:30:15,439 --> 00:30:18,942
It's not a landline.
You're not in a phone booth.

426
00:30:18,942 --> 00:30:21,153
You got to wait for a decent signal.

427
00:30:23,613 --> 00:30:27,242
Violence and technology,
not good bedfellows.

428
00:30:28,368 --> 00:30:30,120
The kind of documentation
Hammond wants

429
00:30:30,120 --> 00:30:33,165
is you and your equipment in the field
as close to the animals as safely possible.

430
00:30:33,165 --> 00:30:34,583
Yeah, that's a great idea.
And while you're at it,

431
00:30:34,583 --> 00:30:36,543
why don't you smear yourself
with a little sheep's blood?

432
00:30:36,543 --> 00:30:41,298
Eddie, is there any reason to think that
the radio in the trailer might work?

433
00:30:41,298 --> 00:30:42,966
Don't tease me.

434
00:30:42,966 --> 00:30:46,595
If you feel qualified at all,
you might try flicking the switch to on.

435
00:30:48,096 --> 00:30:51,141
Okay, I'm taking my daughter
out of here.

436
00:30:51,141 --> 00:30:54,478
Anybody who wants to come with me,
this is your last chance to get out.

437
00:30:54,478 --> 00:30:57,356
When you're out in the field...

438
00:30:57,356 --> 00:30:59,816
Nick, if you're staying,
I'd be happy to deliver

439
00:30:59,816 --> 00:31:03,487
a letter to your wife or loved one
so you can say goodbye.

440
00:31:03,487 --> 00:31:06,573
Eddie, if you have
any personal effects.

441
00:31:06,573 --> 00:31:09,659
It's the least I can do.
I'll be in there, guys.

442
00:31:09,659 --> 00:31:12,454
Our presence needs to be
100% antiseptic.

443
00:31:12,454 --> 00:31:14,915
If we so much as bend
a blade of grass...

444
00:31:14,915 --> 00:31:17,793
Dad, are you mad?
No, I'm not mad. I'm furious.

445
00:31:19,503 --> 00:31:21,630
What is this?
This looks like your room.

446
00:31:21,630 --> 00:31:23,757
I was going to clean it up.
Right now.

447
00:31:25,801 --> 00:31:28,678
What is this?
There are 100 switches!

448
00:31:28,678 --> 00:31:32,849
Ian, don't be mad. I was going to
call to let you know where I was.

449
00:31:32,849 --> 00:31:35,143
I always do, don't I?

450
00:31:35,143 --> 00:31:38,188
Come on. I'm the best kind
of girlfriend there is.

451
00:31:38,188 --> 00:31:41,149
One who travels a lot.
You like that, right?

452
00:31:41,149 --> 00:31:43,610
You love your independence.
Well, I've gotten used to being apart,

453
00:31:43,610 --> 00:31:47,030
but that doesn't mean that
that's how I want to live.

454
00:31:47,030 --> 00:31:51,785
Kelly, this is tall talk.
Just for a minute.

455
00:31:51,785 --> 00:31:54,830
It's like a height restriction
at an amusement park.

456
00:31:55,622 --> 00:31:57,290
If you wanted to rescue me
from something,

457
00:31:57,290 --> 00:32:00,127
why didn't you bail me out
of that fundraiser at the museum

458
00:32:00,127 --> 00:32:02,504
three weeks ago,
like you said you would?

459
00:32:03,964 --> 00:32:06,174
This is a slightly
different situation.

460
00:32:06,174 --> 00:32:10,011
What about the dinner with your parents
you never showed up for?

461
00:32:10,011 --> 00:32:12,806
Why not rescue me
when I really need it?

462
00:32:12,806 --> 00:32:15,142
Be there when you say you will?

463
00:32:15,142 --> 00:32:18,770
I have made a career
out of waiting for you.

464
00:32:18,770 --> 00:32:21,231
You know, Sarah has a pretty good...

465
00:32:21,231 --> 00:32:23,567
It's so important to your future
that you not finish that sentence.

466
00:32:23,567 --> 00:32:25,819
Please, outside!

467
00:32:27,487 --> 00:32:30,323
Ian, come on. Look.

468
00:32:32,534 --> 00:32:36,413
I love that you rode in here
on a white horse. I really do.

469
00:32:37,747 --> 00:32:41,001
It's very touching, very dramatic.

470
00:32:41,585 --> 00:32:44,838
I just need you to show up in a cab
every once in a while, too.

471
00:32:44,838 --> 00:32:47,883
What are you doing?
Don't go out there. It's not safe.

472
00:32:47,883 --> 00:32:50,844
Stay in here. Come back.
Shut the door.

473
00:32:55,265 --> 00:32:56,516
Okay.

474
00:32:57,767 --> 00:33:01,521
I know what I'm doing.
You guys should definitely go.

475
00:33:02,522 --> 00:33:04,191
But I'm going to stay.

476
00:33:04,774 --> 00:33:08,111
I love you. I just don't
need you right now.

477
00:33:09,613 --> 00:33:13,116
What you need is
a good anti-psychotic.

478
00:33:13,116 --> 00:33:15,285
I'll be back in five or six days.

479
00:33:15,285 --> 00:33:16,953
No, you'll be back
in five or six pieces.

480
00:33:16,953 --> 00:33:20,290
What bothers you is that I'm not afraid
of this place, and you are.

481
00:33:20,290 --> 00:33:24,294
Of course I am. That's the whole thing.
All I want is...

482
00:33:24,294 --> 00:33:25,712
What's that sound?

483
00:33:35,847 --> 00:33:38,600
Here we go. I'll get you
out on one of these, now.

484
00:33:38,600 --> 00:33:40,310
Hello! Over here!

485
00:33:43,647 --> 00:33:47,484
I don't get it. It says InGen
on the side of that chopper!

486
00:33:48,276 --> 00:33:51,613
I don't get that. Why would
Hammond send two teams?

487
00:33:53,740 --> 00:33:55,992
Cut the umbilical, Dad!

488
00:33:55,992 --> 00:33:59,120
Doesn't he trust us?
We haven't even started.

489
00:34:08,088 --> 00:34:10,507
This is as good a place as any
for base camp.

490
00:34:10,507 --> 00:34:13,176
That's first priority
after we're finished.

491
00:34:13,176 --> 00:34:17,305
I want it up and running in 30 minutes.
That's half an hour.

492
00:34:17,305 --> 00:34:18,682
Understood? Over.

493
00:34:18,682 --> 00:34:20,141
Cancel that order.

494
00:34:20,141 --> 00:34:22,018
What? Why?

495
00:34:22,018 --> 00:34:23,770
This is a game trail, Mr. Ludlow.

496
00:34:23,770 --> 00:34:25,522
Carnivores hunt on game trails.

497
00:34:25,522 --> 00:34:28,275
You want to set up
base camp or a buffet?

498
00:34:31,778 --> 00:34:35,490
Let's find a new spot, shall we?
Over and out.

499
00:34:35,490 --> 00:34:39,786
Peter, if I run your little camping trip,
there are two conditions.

500
00:34:39,786 --> 00:34:42,956
First, I'm in charge.
If I'm not around, Dieter is.

501
00:34:42,956 --> 00:34:45,959
You just pay us,
tell us we're doing well,

502
00:34:45,959 --> 00:34:48,128
and serve us Scotch after a good day.

503
00:34:48,128 --> 00:34:51,131
Second condition, my fee.
You can keep it.

504
00:34:51,131 --> 00:34:55,010
All I want for my services,
is the right to hunt a Tyrannosaurus.

505
00:34:55,010 --> 00:34:57,887
A male. A buck only.
How and why are my business.

506
00:34:57,887 --> 00:35:00,974
If you don't like those conditions,
you're on your own.

507
00:35:00,974 --> 00:35:03,143
So go ahead, set up base camp here,

508
00:35:03,143 --> 00:35:06,396
in a swamp, or in the middle of
a Rex nest for all I care.

509
00:35:06,396 --> 00:35:09,316
I've had too many safaris
with rich dentists

510
00:35:09,316 --> 00:35:11,526
to listen to any more suicidal ideas.

511
00:35:11,526 --> 00:35:12,652
Okay?

512
00:35:14,404 --> 00:35:15,822
Okay.

513
00:35:20,619 --> 00:35:24,372
Cycle, break off the stray from the herd
and flush him to the right.

514
00:35:24,372 --> 00:35:28,335
Snaggers, stay ready. He's bringing
them out to you. It'll be a...

515
00:35:30,128 --> 00:35:33,590
Pachy... Pachya...
Oh, hell.

516
00:35:33,590 --> 00:35:36,092
The fat head with the bald spot.
Friar Tuck.

517
00:36:00,617 --> 00:36:02,702
Stop.

518
00:36:04,245 --> 00:36:05,830
Pachycephalosaurus.

519
00:36:05,830 --> 00:36:07,290
Carnivore?

520
00:36:07,290 --> 00:36:10,085
No, herbivore, late Cretaceous.

521
00:36:17,217 --> 00:36:19,928
See the distinctive domed skull?

522
00:36:19,928 --> 00:36:22,806
Nine inches of solid bone.
Now, careful.

523
00:36:23,973 --> 00:36:27,310
See, the Pachy's neck attaches
at the bottom of its skull,

524
00:36:27,310 --> 00:36:29,854
instead of at the back of its head,
as with reptiles.

525
00:36:29,854 --> 00:36:33,775
When it lowers its head, its neck
lines up directly with its backbone,

526
00:36:33,775 --> 00:36:36,403
which is perfect
for absorbing impact.

527
00:36:42,659 --> 00:36:44,494
Snagger, Friar Tuck's on the loose,

528
00:36:44,494 --> 00:36:46,496
just about to cross your path.

529
00:37:53,146 --> 00:37:54,773
Dieter, get in the outrigger.

530
00:37:54,773 --> 00:37:57,567
You're closing in on a...

531
00:37:58,359 --> 00:38:00,653
Say that again, Roland.
A what?

532
00:38:01,988 --> 00:38:05,408
The one with the big red horn.
The pompadour. Elvis.

533
00:38:07,952 --> 00:38:09,662
Take me out.

534
00:38:24,969 --> 00:38:26,554
Okay, Carter, reel me in.

535
00:38:31,434 --> 00:38:32,602
Easy!

536
00:38:34,938 --> 00:38:36,356
Not too close.

537
00:38:37,273 --> 00:38:41,069
Go for the legs!
The legs! Watch the tail!

538
00:39:29,158 --> 00:39:30,159
Burke!

539
00:39:30,994 --> 00:39:32,328
Come here.

540
00:39:37,667 --> 00:39:39,502
You recognize this trackway?

541
00:39:39,502 --> 00:39:41,880
Yes, I do. Tyrannosaur.

542
00:39:46,759 --> 00:39:48,011
Roland.

543
00:39:55,226 --> 00:39:57,103
Where do you think you're going?

544
00:39:57,103 --> 00:40:00,356
To collect my fee, Mr. Ludlow.
To collect my fee.

545
00:40:13,786 --> 00:40:14,662
Oh, my God.

546
00:40:15,997 --> 00:40:20,585
Procompsognathus Triassicus,
found by Frass in Bavaria in 1913.

547
00:40:20,585 --> 00:40:22,211
Is it dangerous?

548
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:25,632
No, I wouldn't think so.

549
00:40:26,382 --> 00:40:29,886
The Compy's been presumed
to be a scavengers, like jackals.

550
00:40:30,762 --> 00:40:34,265
It gives me the creeps.
It's like it's not scared.

551
00:40:35,642 --> 00:40:37,936
There haven't been any visitors
to this island.

552
00:40:37,936 --> 00:40:40,229
There's no reason for it to fear man.

553
00:40:44,776 --> 00:40:46,027
Now it does.

554
00:41:06,589 --> 00:41:08,049
It's the Rex nest.

555
00:41:10,385 --> 00:41:12,762
Infant's probably only
a couple weeks old.

556
00:41:12,762 --> 00:41:14,514
Never left the nest.

557
00:41:19,227 --> 00:41:23,398
Offspring that young, the parents
won't leave him alone for too long.

558
00:41:24,774 --> 00:41:27,694
Make your blind here,
wait for the buck to return.

559
00:41:29,070 --> 00:41:32,740
No, if the nest is upwind,
then so are we.

560
00:41:33,491 --> 00:41:38,204
When he comes back, he's gonna know
we're here without us having a chance.

561
00:41:39,122 --> 00:41:40,915
The trick is...

562
00:41:41,666 --> 00:41:45,837
to get him to come
where we want him.

563
00:42:35,094 --> 00:42:37,847
This is why Hammond
was in such a hurry to get you here.

564
00:42:37,847 --> 00:42:39,682
He knew they were coming.

565
00:42:39,682 --> 00:42:41,976
My God, they are well-organized.

566
00:42:43,144 --> 00:42:45,229
Those are some major-league toys.

567
00:42:46,022 --> 00:42:48,107
Maybe I should ask
to use their phone.

568
00:42:48,107 --> 00:42:50,109
Their dish is bigger than yours.

569
00:42:50,109 --> 00:42:53,362
So they actually want to
build another park here?

570
00:42:53,362 --> 00:42:56,157
After what you said happened
on the other island?

571
00:42:56,157 --> 00:42:57,366
They're not building anything.

572
00:42:57,366 --> 00:42:59,660
They're taking these animals
out of here.

573
00:42:59,660 --> 00:43:00,953
Back to the mainland.

574
00:43:02,705 --> 00:43:04,582
I think I should tell you, guys.

575
00:43:04,582 --> 00:43:06,793
Hammond told me these people
might show up.

576
00:43:06,793 --> 00:43:09,754
He thought we'd be finished
by the time they got started,

577
00:43:09,754 --> 00:43:11,964
but in case they weren't,
he did send a backup plan.

578
00:43:11,964 --> 00:43:13,382
What backup plan?

579
00:43:14,300 --> 00:43:15,343
Me.

580
00:43:16,094 --> 00:43:18,805
Simply put, InGen is seeking
limited partners

581
00:43:18,805 --> 00:43:21,224
to defray some of our expenses.

582
00:43:21,224 --> 00:43:24,393
The prospectus you've been given
by the board details

583
00:43:24,393 --> 00:43:27,188
our projected hardware
and construction expenses.

584
00:43:27,188 --> 00:43:29,690
As you can see
by my two friends here,

585
00:43:29,690 --> 00:43:32,193
the software is already
fully developed.

586
00:43:35,488 --> 00:43:38,407
One might say,
it'd be up and running.

587
00:43:40,159 --> 00:43:43,246
In a moment, I'll take you
on a stroll through the camp

588
00:43:43,246 --> 00:43:45,915
and you'll see some larger,
more impressive specimens.

589
00:44:14,569 --> 00:44:18,614
You don't bring people halfway
around the world to visit a zoo.

590
00:44:18,614 --> 00:44:20,908
You bring the zoo to them.

591
00:44:20,908 --> 00:44:23,077
San Diego is the perfect setting.

592
00:44:23,619 --> 00:44:27,456
People already associate our
beautiful city with animal attractions.

593
00:44:28,249 --> 00:44:32,962
San Diego Zoo, Sea World,
San Diego Chargers.

594
00:45:00,865 --> 00:45:02,491
Mr. Hammond knew this.

595
00:45:03,409 --> 00:45:05,745
Before he ever dreamt of an island

596
00:45:06,996 --> 00:45:10,124
he began construction
on an amphitheatre,

597
00:45:10,791 --> 00:45:14,795
very near to where you're sitting now,
the InGen Waterfront Complex.

598
00:45:15,588 --> 00:45:17,465
But he abandoned it

599
00:45:18,341 --> 00:45:21,761
in favor of something far grander

600
00:45:21,761 --> 00:45:24,096
and ultimately impossible.

601
00:45:25,306 --> 00:45:28,935
And so, the facility sits unused,
unfinished,

602
00:45:29,685 --> 00:45:31,979
when it could be completed and ready

603
00:45:31,979 --> 00:45:34,148
to receive visitors
in less than a month.

604
00:45:56,796 --> 00:45:58,881
Ajay, look out! Jump!

605
00:46:26,951 --> 00:46:29,578
That's the last time
I leave you in charge.

606
00:46:52,226 --> 00:46:54,437
Oh, my God.
Are you out of your mind?

607
00:46:54,437 --> 00:46:55,813
She's got a broken leg.

608
00:46:55,813 --> 00:46:57,857
Let's get in the car
before they hear us.

609
00:46:57,857 --> 00:46:59,608
You have any idea what that is?

610
00:46:59,608 --> 00:47:01,152
Come on, open the door.

611
00:47:01,944 --> 00:47:03,154
You're nuts.

612
00:47:05,531 --> 00:47:08,451
Man, Ian's not going to like this.

613
00:47:12,663 --> 00:47:14,415
What's going on?

614
00:47:14,415 --> 00:47:15,833
Isn't it obvious?

615
00:47:18,961 --> 00:47:21,047
We're not alone on this island.

616
00:47:31,015 --> 00:47:33,100
It's not set to the frequency.

617
00:47:36,645 --> 00:47:38,439
Look here.

618
00:47:56,123 --> 00:47:58,000
What the hell is that?

619
00:47:59,085 --> 00:48:00,753
It's right here. Hello?

620
00:48:06,008 --> 00:48:09,428
No, not Enrique. Ian Malcolm.
Are you on the boat?

621
00:48:10,763 --> 00:48:12,390
No, is this the boat?

622
00:48:14,058 --> 00:48:18,604
We're on Isla Sorna. We need to
talk to the boat, Mar del Plata.

623
00:48:25,694 --> 00:48:26,946
I'll get him.

624
00:48:27,488 --> 00:48:29,907
All right. That's it.
I got you.

625
00:48:31,075 --> 00:48:32,660
I got you.
Hurry up.

626
00:48:32,660 --> 00:48:36,747
Let's go. Go. Come on.
Watch his tail. Get the door.

627
00:48:39,208 --> 00:48:40,543
Boy, is she mad at you.

628
00:48:40,543 --> 00:48:42,211
I feel sorry for that guy, Enrique.

629
00:48:42,211 --> 00:48:43,462
No lectures, please.

630
00:48:43,462 --> 00:48:46,757
Honey, stay back.
Don't stand up, okay?

631
00:48:46,757 --> 00:48:48,509
I got it!
Watch his head.

632
00:48:49,301 --> 00:48:50,469
Watch his head.

633
00:48:50,469 --> 00:48:51,554
Ian, he's hurt!

634
00:48:52,513 --> 00:48:53,722
No, honey, no.

635
00:48:53,722 --> 00:48:55,057
I'm going to get the table.

636
00:48:55,057 --> 00:48:58,811
He'll snip at you. Watch his tail,
he's swinging his tail.

637
00:48:59,937 --> 00:49:01,814
Set him down.

638
00:49:01,814 --> 00:49:03,315
All right?
Okay, I got him.

639
00:49:03,315 --> 00:49:04,608
Got him?
Yeah.

640
00:49:06,068 --> 00:49:08,154
It's okay.
There you go.

641
00:49:08,154 --> 00:49:10,656
What must I do, hit her with a stick?

642
00:49:10,656 --> 00:49:12,324
No, don't get close, honey.

643
00:49:12,324 --> 00:49:13,576
Watch out!
Don't stand too close.

644
00:49:13,576 --> 00:49:14,493
He smells.

645
00:49:17,538 --> 00:49:22,042
No, lady, enough!
Wrong frequency. God.

646
00:49:25,713 --> 00:49:28,549
Nick, don't let him get
those things around you.

647
00:49:31,260 --> 00:49:33,554
Hello, it's Ian Malcolm.

648
00:49:34,638 --> 00:49:36,724
Okay, let's take a look at this.

649
00:49:37,558 --> 00:49:40,102
Easy, easy.
Just calm down now.

650
00:49:42,354 --> 00:49:44,398
Okay, here are the metatarsals.

651
00:49:49,653 --> 00:49:52,948
Tibia, fibula. There it is.
There's the fracture.

652
00:49:52,948 --> 00:49:53,949
Right above the epiphysis.

653
00:49:53,949 --> 00:49:54,950
How bad is it?

654
00:49:54,950 --> 00:49:57,453
If we don't set it, he'll die.
It won't heal.

655
00:49:57,453 --> 00:49:59,705
He won't be able to pivot
on his ankle.

656
00:49:59,705 --> 00:50:01,332
He won't be able to run or walk.

657
00:50:01,332 --> 00:50:03,542
A predator will pick him off.

658
00:50:04,668 --> 00:50:06,670
Carlos, come in.

659
00:50:06,670 --> 00:50:08,964
God damn it, you bastard, listen...

660
00:50:09,840 --> 00:50:12,051
Other animals are going to hear this.

661
00:50:12,051 --> 00:50:14,470
Come in, please.
Can you hear me?

662
00:50:14,470 --> 00:50:16,805
I've got to get out of here.
I want to leave.

663
00:50:16,805 --> 00:50:17,973
I'm calling the boat.

664
00:50:17,973 --> 00:50:19,433
I want to go someplace else.

665
00:50:19,433 --> 00:50:22,228
I don't want to be here.
I want to be somewhere safe.

666
00:50:22,228 --> 00:50:23,479
Isn't it safe here?

667
00:50:23,479 --> 00:50:25,689
I want to be somewhere high.

668
00:50:25,689 --> 00:50:28,901
What? What is it?
What's going on?

669
00:50:29,401 --> 00:50:31,278
Information, please.
What?

670
00:50:31,278 --> 00:50:35,032
You're much happier not knowing.
Get us up. Here we go.

671
00:50:36,492 --> 00:50:38,202
There we go.

672
00:50:40,120 --> 00:50:44,041
I'm so stupid. I shouldn't
have come along with you.

673
00:50:44,041 --> 00:50:45,417
Can you set it?

674
00:50:45,417 --> 00:50:47,419
Yeah, I just need something temporary

675
00:50:47,419 --> 00:50:50,005
to break apart and fall off
as he grows.

676
00:50:51,090 --> 00:50:54,009
Whenever you're ready.
He's fighting, Dr. Quinn.

677
00:50:58,722 --> 00:51:01,016
What's the frequency for the boat?

678
00:51:01,016 --> 00:51:03,519
15888.
It's third from the top.

679
00:51:05,729 --> 00:51:10,192
Okay, we're high, and this is
the safest place you can be.

680
00:51:10,192 --> 00:51:12,361
Like Sarah said before,
these plants make it

681
00:51:12,361 --> 00:51:14,238
so the animals
won't even know you're here.

682
00:51:14,238 --> 00:51:16,031
You're just trying
to make me feel better.

683
00:51:16,031 --> 00:51:18,367
I remember all those stories
you told.

684
00:51:18,367 --> 00:51:20,327
Oh, no, no.
This is nothing like that.

685
00:51:20,327 --> 00:51:23,414
We're in a completely
different situation right now.

686
00:51:33,048 --> 00:51:36,010
Is there any way we can communicate
with the trailer?

687
00:51:40,097 --> 00:51:41,807
He's moving again, more morphine.

688
00:51:41,807 --> 00:51:45,436
We have no idea what his metabolism is.
We'll kill him with too much.

689
00:51:46,729 --> 00:51:49,857
I need your hand here.
Put some pressure there.

690
00:51:49,857 --> 00:51:53,277
No answer. What a surprise.
How do I get down?

691
00:51:53,902 --> 00:51:56,113
Tie this belt around you.

692
00:51:56,113 --> 00:51:57,906
Where are you going?

693
00:51:57,906 --> 00:52:00,075
Stay here. Come on,
stay here. Please!

694
00:52:00,075 --> 00:52:01,910
Squeeze this rope hard.

695
00:52:01,910 --> 00:52:04,246
The harder you squeeze,
the slower you go.

696
00:52:04,246 --> 00:52:05,664
If you don't squeeze at all...

697
00:52:05,664 --> 00:52:07,082
I get the idea.

698
00:52:07,082 --> 00:52:08,626
Dad, please stay here.

699
00:52:09,168 --> 00:52:11,045
Stay here, please.

700
00:52:11,045 --> 00:52:12,796
Honey, the queen?
The goddess?

701
00:52:14,256 --> 00:52:15,424
Your inspiration?

702
00:52:15,424 --> 00:52:16,467
Now you're talking.

703
00:52:16,467 --> 00:52:18,802
Now you're talking.
All right.

704
00:52:22,097 --> 00:52:25,225
I'm coming right back.
I give you my word.

705
00:52:25,225 --> 00:52:27,311
But you never keep your word!

706
00:52:33,609 --> 00:52:36,612
I would have squeezed
just a little bit harder.

707
00:52:36,612 --> 00:52:38,989
Okay, I'm almost finished.

708
00:52:38,989 --> 00:52:41,200
Damn it.
I need another adhesive.

709
00:52:41,200 --> 00:52:42,826
Something pliable that I can...

710
00:52:46,789 --> 00:52:47,998
Spit.

711
00:52:50,876 --> 00:52:52,044
Your gum.

712
00:53:30,124 --> 00:53:33,085
Get the amoxicillin
and fill a syringe.

713
00:53:33,085 --> 00:53:35,963
Quick injection of antibiotics
and he's out of here.

714
00:53:41,927 --> 00:53:44,930
For once in your life,
would it kill you to pick it up?

715
00:53:44,930 --> 00:53:46,181
Help me get it out of here.

716
00:53:46,181 --> 00:53:48,517
No, we just set its leg.

717
00:53:53,480 --> 00:53:55,482
Mommy's very angry.

718
00:55:11,934 --> 00:55:16,146
This isn't hunting behavior, Ian.
They're searching.

719
00:55:17,064 --> 00:55:18,899
They came for their infant.

720
00:55:20,317 --> 00:55:22,069
Let's not disappoint them.

721
00:55:23,987 --> 00:55:26,281
Let me get his head, okay?

722
00:55:27,407 --> 00:55:29,117
Careful. Be careful.

723
00:55:30,619 --> 00:55:32,287
I got him.

724
00:55:32,287 --> 00:55:33,580
Muzzle off.

725
00:56:13,495 --> 00:56:14,872
Yes, Eddie?

726
00:56:14,872 --> 00:56:17,374
They're going back to the jungle.

727
00:56:18,375 --> 00:56:20,335
I know. I see.
How's Kelly?

728
00:56:20,335 --> 00:56:22,212
She's fine.
She's right here.

729
00:56:23,839 --> 00:56:25,048
Hello?

730
00:56:25,048 --> 00:56:27,009
Kelly, are you okay?

731
00:56:27,009 --> 00:56:28,051
Yeah, I'm good.

732
00:56:28,844 --> 00:56:33,181
Stay there. Don't move.
I'll be right back up. Understand?

733
00:56:33,181 --> 00:56:34,683
Yeah, I understand.

734
00:56:37,686 --> 00:56:39,521
I beg people to listen to me.

735
00:56:39,521 --> 00:56:41,690
I use plain, simple English.

736
00:56:41,690 --> 00:56:42,983
Shut up.

737
00:56:42,983 --> 00:56:45,944
That should make an interesting
chapter in your book.

738
00:56:45,944 --> 00:56:48,030
The debate over
the parental instincts

739
00:56:48,030 --> 00:56:50,657
of the T-Rex is now academic.

740
00:56:57,414 --> 00:56:59,625
Hang on.
This is gonna be bad.

741
00:57:18,226 --> 00:57:20,729
They're pushing us over the cliff.

742
00:57:20,729 --> 00:57:22,105
Oh, my God.

743
00:57:28,779 --> 00:57:30,405
Come on, come on.

744
00:57:30,405 --> 00:57:31,573
Once we're out of here...

745
00:57:31,573 --> 00:57:32,616
Into the woods.

746
00:57:32,616 --> 00:57:35,118
Straight into the woods.
Come on!

747
00:57:35,118 --> 00:57:36,578
This is where they hit us.

748
00:57:43,543 --> 00:57:45,963
Hang on to something!

749
00:58:05,190 --> 00:58:06,108
Sarah!

750
00:58:25,794 --> 00:58:28,255
Oh, God, please!

751
00:58:34,177 --> 00:58:35,303
Don't move!

752
00:58:36,304 --> 00:58:39,266
I'm coming down for you.
Here I come. Stay still.

753
00:58:40,642 --> 00:58:42,144
Here I come, Sarah.

754
00:58:51,361 --> 00:58:53,321
The satellite phone, get it!

755
00:58:55,824 --> 00:58:56,908
Okay.

756
00:59:26,688 --> 00:59:28,940
Give me your hand, honey.

757
00:59:33,945 --> 00:59:35,864
Heads up!

758
00:59:48,543 --> 00:59:50,170
Your lucky pack.

759
00:59:55,717 --> 00:59:57,094
I got her.

760
01:00:28,375 --> 01:00:29,709
Nick?
We're in here.

761
01:00:29,709 --> 01:00:31,002
Hold on!

762
01:00:33,213 --> 01:00:35,423
I'm coming.
God damn it!

763
01:00:39,928 --> 01:00:41,721
I'm coming.

764
01:00:41,721 --> 01:00:43,223
What did this?

765
01:00:43,223 --> 01:00:45,809
Kelly. What'd you do with Kelly?

766
01:00:45,809 --> 01:00:47,978
She's okay.
She's in the High Hide.

767
01:00:49,062 --> 01:00:50,730
Who's hurt? What do you need?

768
01:00:50,730 --> 01:00:52,065
We need rope.

769
01:00:52,065 --> 01:00:53,650
Rope. Anything else?

770
01:00:54,276 --> 01:00:56,027
Three cheeseburgers with everything.

771
01:00:56,027 --> 01:00:56,945
No onions on mine.

772
01:00:56,945 --> 01:00:58,947
And an apple turnover.

773
01:01:37,027 --> 01:01:38,111
Hold on.

774
01:01:40,322 --> 01:01:41,489
I'm coming!

775
01:01:42,157 --> 01:01:43,992
Bring the rope.
Let's go.

776
01:01:43,992 --> 01:01:45,994
I'm coming.
Now, now.

777
01:01:45,994 --> 01:01:47,329
Eddie, you all right?

778
01:01:47,329 --> 01:01:48,830
Yeah. I'm coming.

779
01:01:48,830 --> 01:01:51,791
We need the rope.
Throw it down here. Hurry up!

780
01:01:51,791 --> 01:01:54,211
Good throw.
Did you tie this to anything?

781
01:01:58,798 --> 01:02:01,509
We're sliding down!

782
01:02:15,649 --> 01:02:17,525
Up you go.

783
01:03:35,395 --> 01:03:38,481
Eddie! Hey!

784
01:04:31,201 --> 01:04:32,160
Go.

785
01:04:32,994 --> 01:04:34,871
Increase your rate of climb.

786
01:06:56,429 --> 01:06:59,807
Our communication equipment's
been destroyed.

787
01:06:59,807 --> 01:07:03,853
And if your radio and satellite phone
were in those trailers...

788
01:07:03,853 --> 01:07:04,979
They were.

789
01:07:04,979 --> 01:07:06,481
Then we're stuck here.

790
01:07:06,481 --> 01:07:08,900
And stuck together,
thanks to you people.

791
01:07:08,900 --> 01:07:11,819
We came to watch.
You came to strip-mine the place.

792
01:07:11,819 --> 01:07:14,155
Back off.
At least we came prepared.

793
01:07:15,782 --> 01:07:17,784
Five years of work and a hundred miles
of electrified fence

794
01:07:17,784 --> 01:07:19,661
couldn't prepare the other island,
and you think that, what,

795
01:07:19,661 --> 01:07:21,538
a couple dozen Marlboro men
were going to make a difference?

796
01:07:21,538 --> 01:07:24,624
It's a looter mentality. All you care about
is what you can take. You have no right.

797
01:07:24,624 --> 01:07:27,502
An extinct animal brought
back to life has no rights.

798
01:07:27,502 --> 01:07:29,003
It exists because we made it.

799
01:07:29,003 --> 01:07:30,797
We patented it. We own it.

800
01:07:31,589 --> 01:07:33,341
You looking for a problem?

801
01:07:33,341 --> 01:07:34,884
And I found you, didn't I?

802
01:07:35,969 --> 01:07:37,345
Stop!

803
01:07:38,555 --> 01:07:40,098
Come on!

804
01:07:40,098 --> 01:07:41,849
I know you.

805
01:07:41,849 --> 01:07:44,185
You're that Earth First bastard.

806
01:07:44,185 --> 01:07:46,521
What's Earth First?
Professional saboteurs.

807
01:07:46,521 --> 01:07:47,855
Environmentalists!
Criminals.

808
01:07:47,855 --> 01:07:49,983
Knock it off!
Listen to me.

809
01:07:49,983 --> 01:07:51,859
By moving the baby rex
into our camp

810
01:07:51,859 --> 01:07:54,028
we may have changed
the adults' perceived territory.

811
01:07:54,028 --> 01:07:55,989
Their what?

812
01:07:55,989 --> 01:07:58,032
That's why they persisted
in destroying the trailers.

813
01:07:58,032 --> 01:08:00,076
They now feel they have to
defend this entire area.

814
01:08:00,076 --> 01:08:01,828
We have to move right now.

815
01:08:01,828 --> 01:08:03,997
Move where?
Our boat, their airlift,

816
01:08:03,997 --> 01:08:05,707
they're both waiting for an order
we have no way to send.

817
01:08:05,707 --> 01:08:07,750
All right. All right.

818
01:08:07,750 --> 01:08:12,005
There's a communications center here
near the old operations building.

819
01:08:12,005 --> 01:08:14,424
Hammond ran everything
on geothermal power.

820
01:08:14,424 --> 01:08:16,593
It was never meant
to need replenishing.

821
01:08:16,593 --> 01:08:19,804
If we can get here, we can send
a radio call to the airlift.

822
01:08:19,804 --> 01:08:21,806
You have the frequency
written down somewhere?

823
01:08:21,806 --> 01:08:24,183
Right here in this book.
We'll mind those.

824
01:08:24,183 --> 01:08:25,893
How far is the village?

825
01:08:25,893 --> 01:08:28,187
A day's walk, maybe more.

826
01:08:31,399 --> 01:08:33,109
That's not the problem.

827
01:08:33,109 --> 01:08:34,444
What is?

828
01:08:34,444 --> 01:08:36,821
What is the problem?

829
01:08:37,530 --> 01:08:39,032
Velociraptors.

830
01:08:44,370 --> 01:08:47,999
Our infrared show their nesting sites
are concentrated in the island's interior,

831
01:08:47,999 --> 01:08:50,710
which is why we were planning to
keep to the outer rim.

832
01:08:50,710 --> 01:08:52,337
What's a Veloc...

833
01:08:52,337 --> 01:08:56,007
Velociraptor.
Carnivore. Pack hunter.

834
01:08:56,007 --> 01:08:58,301
About two meters tall, long snout,

835
01:08:58,301 --> 01:09:02,555
binocular vision, strong dexterous
forearms and killing claws on both feet.

836
01:09:02,555 --> 01:09:05,016
And the Rexes may continue
to track us too,

837
01:09:05,016 --> 01:09:07,185
if they feel we're a threat
to them or their infant.

838
01:09:07,185 --> 01:09:10,480
No, no, you're wrong there, Dr. Harding,
we'll lose them once we leave their territory.

839
01:09:10,480 --> 01:09:12,106
No, don't bet on it.

840
01:09:12,106 --> 01:09:14,942
Tyrannosaur has got the largest
proportional olfactory cavity

841
01:09:14,942 --> 01:09:18,071
of any creature in the fossil record,
with the exception of one.

842
01:09:18,071 --> 01:09:19,405
Right.

843
01:09:19,405 --> 01:09:23,034
The turkey vulture
could scent up to 10 miles.

844
01:09:23,034 --> 01:09:25,495
Right. This is all very thrilling,

845
01:09:25,495 --> 01:09:27,497
but I say we should push on
to the village.

846
01:09:27,497 --> 01:09:29,707
We could head back down
to the lagoon.

847
01:09:29,707 --> 01:09:31,959
And dow what?
Sit out in the open,

848
01:09:31,959 --> 01:09:36,464
next to a heavily used water source
and hope that your captain comes back?

849
01:09:36,464 --> 01:09:39,050
He won't do that. He knows better.
Then we head for the village,

850
01:09:39,050 --> 01:09:42,261
we might find some shelter
and we can call for help.

851
01:09:42,261 --> 01:09:45,056
Rex just fed,
so he won't stalk us for food.

852
01:09:45,056 --> 01:09:47,016
Just fed? I assume
you're talking about Eddie?

853
01:09:47,016 --> 01:09:50,019
You might show a little repsect.
The man saved our lives by giving his.

854
01:09:50,019 --> 01:09:51,729
Then his troubles are over.

855
01:09:51,729 --> 01:09:54,649
My point is that predators don't hunt
when they're not hungry.

856
01:09:54,649 --> 01:09:57,151
No, only humans do.
Oh, you're breaking our heart.

857
01:09:57,151 --> 01:10:00,321
Saddle up! Let's get this
moveable feast underway.

858
01:10:17,505 --> 01:10:19,006
Excuse me, cowboy.

859
01:10:20,508 --> 01:10:22,009
You want some gum?

860
01:10:24,387 --> 01:10:26,681
You seem like you have
a shred of common sense.

861
01:10:26,681 --> 01:10:28,683
What the hell are you doing here?

862
01:10:29,851 --> 01:10:33,354
Somewhere on this island is the greatest
predator that ever lived.

863
01:10:33,354 --> 01:10:36,190
The second greatest predator
must take him down.

864
01:10:36,190 --> 01:10:37,608
You going to use that?

865
01:10:37,608 --> 01:10:39,485
If he doesn't surrender, yes.

866
01:10:39,485 --> 01:10:41,612
Let me see it for a second.
No.

867
01:10:43,322 --> 01:10:45,533
The animal exists on the planet

868
01:10:45,533 --> 01:10:47,410
for the first time
in tens of millions of years

869
01:10:47,410 --> 01:10:50,079
and the only way you can
express yourself is to kill it.

870
01:10:50,079 --> 01:10:53,124
Remember that chap about 20 years ago?
I forget his name.

871
01:10:53,124 --> 01:10:56,502
Climbed Everest without any oxygen.
Came down nearly dead.

872
01:10:56,502 --> 01:10:59,547
When they asked him,
why did you go up there to die?

873
01:10:59,547 --> 01:11:02,216
He said, I didn't.
I went up there to live.

874
01:11:07,513 --> 01:11:10,099
I haven't had a chance to wish you luck
on your new business venture.

875
01:11:10,099 --> 01:11:11,476
You're off to a promising start.

876
01:11:11,476 --> 01:11:14,687
My team is intact, doctor.
I'm sorry for the loss of your man.

877
01:11:14,687 --> 01:11:16,564
You know, it's very easy
to criticize someone

878
01:11:16,564 --> 01:11:19,066
who generates an idea,
someone who assumes all the risk.

879
01:11:19,066 --> 01:11:22,153
When you try to sound like Hammond,
it just comes off like a hustle.

880
01:11:22,153 --> 01:11:25,823
I mean, it's not your fault.
They say talent skips a generation, so...

881
01:11:25,823 --> 01:11:28,701
Hey, I'm sure your kids
are going to be sharp as tacks.

882
01:11:28,701 --> 01:11:32,121
Hammond's reach exceeded his grasp.
Mine does not.

883
01:11:32,121 --> 01:11:34,081
Taking dinosaurs off this island

884
01:11:34,081 --> 01:11:37,794
is the worst idea in the long,
sad history of bad ideas.

885
01:11:37,794 --> 01:11:40,797
And I'm going to be there
when you learn that.

886
01:11:55,937 --> 01:11:57,021
Go.

887
01:12:14,997 --> 01:12:17,500
Take a break. Five minutes.

888
01:12:29,053 --> 01:12:30,721
Are you injured?

889
01:12:33,015 --> 01:12:36,018
No, it's from the baby.
I set its broken leg.

890
01:12:36,018 --> 01:12:37,687
It's just not drying in the humidity.

891
01:12:37,687 --> 01:12:39,814
Roland, a word?

892
01:12:41,315 --> 01:12:43,651
I'd love to know where we are.

893
01:12:48,948 --> 01:12:51,117
Making friends with Ahab, huh?

894
01:13:06,299 --> 01:13:09,051
Hey, Carter, I'm going
to the ladies' room.

895
01:13:09,051 --> 01:13:11,679
Wait for me here. Okay?

896
01:14:00,978 --> 01:14:04,190
It's not polite
to sneak up on people.

897
01:14:24,794 --> 01:14:26,629
Hey, Carter!

898
01:14:27,171 --> 01:14:28,965
Where are you?

899
01:14:30,508 --> 01:14:32,134
Hey, Carter!

900
01:14:32,885 --> 01:14:35,137
I got turned around in here.

901
01:14:37,390 --> 01:14:38,766
Shit!

902
01:14:39,642 --> 01:14:42,603
Hey, Carter, let me hear from you!

903
01:14:49,360 --> 01:14:51,070
Hey, Carter!

904
01:15:06,460 --> 01:15:07,878
Hey, Carter!

905
01:15:49,170 --> 01:15:50,921
Carter!

906
01:16:36,884 --> 01:16:39,095
Hey, Roland!

907
01:16:44,100 --> 01:16:46,602
Okay, break's over. Moving on!

908
01:16:54,485 --> 01:16:55,903
Carry me.

909
01:16:59,240 --> 01:17:01,492
Carter. Carter.

910
01:17:27,226 --> 01:17:28,644
Get off!

911
01:18:29,538 --> 01:18:31,248
When did you last see him?

912
01:18:31,248 --> 01:18:33,417
10, 15 minutes.

913
01:18:35,377 --> 01:18:39,131
Come with me. And you.
If he's alive, we'll find him.

914
01:18:39,131 --> 01:18:41,050
The rest of you keep right on.

915
01:18:41,050 --> 01:18:42,843
Ten minutes,
you should reach the ridge.

916
01:18:42,843 --> 01:18:44,345
Wait for us there.

917
01:18:45,095 --> 01:18:46,472
Miss Harding?

918
01:18:47,431 --> 01:18:49,433
No one tells the little girl.

919
01:18:54,813 --> 01:18:56,148
Right.

920
01:18:56,982 --> 01:18:58,651
Let's carry on.

921
01:19:00,694 --> 01:19:02,696
Everybody up.

922
01:19:04,323 --> 01:19:06,408
Come on. Up, up, up.

923
01:19:07,576 --> 01:19:10,329
All right, guys,
let's get the hell out of here.

924
01:19:12,706 --> 01:19:13,749
Come on.

925
01:19:47,908 --> 01:19:49,326
Roland?

926
01:20:02,172 --> 01:20:03,841
Did you find him?

927
01:20:08,387 --> 01:20:11,223
Just the parts they didn't like.
Map.

928
01:20:14,393 --> 01:20:18,772
The operations building
is right down in there

929
01:20:18,772 --> 01:20:21,734
about a mile and a half
from the base of these cliffs.

930
01:20:21,734 --> 01:20:24,653
How do you know that?
I've seen it.

931
01:20:24,653 --> 01:20:26,822
Now, the climb down won't be easy.

932
01:20:27,906 --> 01:20:30,409
We'll let them sleep one more hour.

933
01:20:31,910 --> 01:20:33,454
And then we hit it.

934
01:21:24,171 --> 01:21:25,589
Oh, no.

935
01:22:42,624 --> 01:22:44,668
What is that?

936
01:23:07,316 --> 01:23:08,609
Kelly.

937
01:23:20,037 --> 01:23:22,247
Stay down! Don't move!

938
01:23:22,247 --> 01:23:23,832
Don't run!

939
01:23:32,049 --> 01:23:34,468
Don't look!
Just run! Run, run, run!

940
01:23:49,483 --> 01:23:50,651
Bastard!

941
01:25:29,249 --> 01:25:30,834
You son of a bitch!

942
01:25:30,834 --> 01:25:32,252
He can't reach you!

943
01:25:32,252 --> 01:25:33,587
Stay back!

944
01:25:35,422 --> 01:25:37,174
He can't reach us!

945
01:25:37,174 --> 01:25:38,091
No!

946
01:25:48,977 --> 01:25:52,814
Oh, my God!
A snake! Help me!

947
01:25:52,814 --> 01:25:54,066
Stay back!

948
01:26:04,785 --> 01:26:08,705
You're okay. You're okay.
It's okay. It's okay.

949
01:26:08,705 --> 01:26:10,290
It's coming back!

950
01:26:14,169 --> 01:26:15,462
Dad!

951
01:26:17,214 --> 01:26:18,632
Thank you.

952
01:26:31,311 --> 01:26:33,897
Don't go into the long grass!

953
01:26:39,403 --> 01:26:41,822
Not into the long grass!

954
01:27:40,547 --> 01:27:41,840
Hey, hey!

955
01:27:45,051 --> 01:27:46,511
Look at this.

956
01:27:46,511 --> 01:27:47,637
What is it?

957
01:27:47,637 --> 01:27:49,347
I think it's Ajay's bag.

958
01:28:05,322 --> 01:28:08,325
Go, as fast as you can.

959
01:28:09,576 --> 01:28:11,119
Go!

960
01:28:42,192 --> 01:28:43,944
I see structures there.

961
01:28:45,111 --> 01:28:48,782
They said the communications center
is in the operations building.

962
01:28:48,782 --> 01:28:52,994
So I'll just get in there and I'll send
the radio call. I'll see you there.

963
01:28:52,994 --> 01:28:54,454
Wait for us.
Every second counts.

964
01:28:54,454 --> 01:28:56,623
If anyone thinks they can keep up,
you're welcome to try.

965
01:28:56,623 --> 01:28:59,626
Okay, here we come.
No! Ian, rest.

966
01:29:47,132 --> 01:29:48,550
Oh, man.

967
01:31:02,624 --> 01:31:03,917
Damn it!

968
01:31:06,169 --> 01:31:07,963
Be there. Be there.

969
01:31:09,339 --> 01:31:13,426
CQ, this is InGen Operations
Harvest Leader to Harvest Base.

970
01:31:13,426 --> 01:31:16,596
Repeating, I'm calling for
InGen Operations Harvest Leader

971
01:31:16,596 --> 01:31:18,682
seeking InGen Harvest Base.

972
01:31:18,682 --> 01:31:20,392
Go ahead, Harvest Leader.

973
01:31:20,392 --> 01:31:22,978
The operation has suffered
severe casualties.

974
01:31:22,978 --> 01:31:25,230
The survivors are now
in mortal danger.

975
01:31:25,230 --> 01:31:27,482
I need you to send rescue
immediately.

976
01:31:27,482 --> 01:31:30,360
Our coordinates here are...

977
01:31:30,360 --> 01:31:34,447
Our coordinates here are 9 degrees
58 minutes north, 85 degrees...

978
01:32:25,582 --> 01:32:26,791
Nick!

979
01:32:38,803 --> 01:32:40,346
Nick Van Owen?

980
01:33:07,832 --> 01:33:10,126
Here, here, here!

981
01:33:10,126 --> 01:33:11,836
Here, here, here!

982
01:33:11,836 --> 01:33:13,004
Inside! Anywhere!

983
01:33:14,714 --> 01:33:16,800
Go, go inside!

984
01:35:18,546 --> 01:35:19,923
Go. Go!

985
01:35:19,923 --> 01:35:22,342
Where do I go when I'm out?
I'm right behind you...

986
01:35:49,702 --> 01:35:52,580
Look out! Look out!
Watch out!

987
01:35:54,707 --> 01:35:56,167
Up here!

988
01:36:07,470 --> 01:36:08,888
Kelly, no!

989
01:36:21,067 --> 01:36:22,652
Hey, you!

990
01:36:43,089 --> 01:36:44,966
The school cut you from the team?

991
01:36:44,966 --> 01:36:48,678
Get out of here!
Get out of here! Go!

992
01:38:08,925 --> 01:38:10,551
Come on. Let's go!

993
01:38:13,179 --> 01:38:15,473
Come on. Let's go!
Let's go! Let's go!

994
01:38:36,702 --> 01:38:38,454
Let's go! Let's go!

995
01:38:38,454 --> 01:38:40,206
What about the others?
There must be more survivors!

996
01:38:40,206 --> 01:38:42,166
There's another chopper coming.

997
01:39:00,726 --> 01:39:02,395
It's okay. It's over now.

998
01:39:04,730 --> 01:39:07,525
That's one souvenir
they won't be taking with them.

999
01:39:16,367 --> 01:39:18,578
Find the infant Tyrannosaur.

1000
01:39:18,578 --> 01:39:20,371
Roland can show you
where the nest is.

1001
01:39:20,371 --> 01:39:21,664
I want it on my jet.

1002
01:39:22,290 --> 01:39:25,960
I'll take it directly to the infirmary
at the complex in San Diego.

1003
01:39:25,960 --> 01:39:27,170
Hurry it up.

1004
01:39:27,170 --> 01:39:30,256
I'd love to be airborne
before the female knows we're here.

1005
01:39:33,968 --> 01:39:36,345
Roland? Roland?

1006
01:39:37,430 --> 01:39:39,307
You probably saved InGen.

1007
01:39:39,307 --> 01:39:41,642
We lost everything
we came after on this trip.

1008
01:39:41,642 --> 01:39:45,396
But this animal and its infant are going
to single-handedly bail us out.

1009
01:39:45,396 --> 01:39:46,772
Congratulations.

1010
01:39:47,440 --> 01:39:49,484
You've got your trophy!

1011
01:39:49,484 --> 01:39:52,528
A buck only. But it's alive!

1012
01:39:52,528 --> 01:39:54,447
And everyone one the planet
is going to line up

1013
01:39:54,447 --> 01:39:56,908
to appreciate it and everything
that you've done for us.

1014
01:39:57,867 --> 01:39:59,619
What's the matter?

1015
01:39:59,619 --> 01:40:01,954
He didn't make it. Ajay.

1016
01:40:03,039 --> 01:40:04,415
I'm sorry.

1017
01:40:05,416 --> 01:40:07,126
Really, I am.

1018
01:40:10,296 --> 01:40:13,216
I remember the people
who've helped me.

1019
01:40:13,216 --> 01:40:16,052
There's a job for you at the park
in San Diego, if you want it.

1020
01:40:16,052 --> 01:40:17,386
No, thank you.

1021
01:40:19,639 --> 01:40:22,558
I believe I've spent enough time
in the company of death.

1022
01:40:36,447 --> 01:40:37,782
Oh, my God.

1023
01:41:38,467 --> 01:41:41,304
Fifteen years ago,
John Hammond had a dream.

1024
01:41:41,929 --> 01:41:44,140
Like John himself,
the dream was grand.

1025
01:41:44,140 --> 01:41:46,309
It was outsized.
It was bold.

1026
01:41:46,309 --> 01:41:50,021
It was impractical.
It was not to be.

1027
01:41:50,021 --> 01:41:52,023
But half an hour from now,

1028
01:41:52,023 --> 01:41:55,526
John Hammond's dream,
re-imagined, will come true.

1029
01:41:56,319 --> 01:41:57,820
For 0.01 of the cost

1030
01:41:57,820 --> 01:42:00,531
of building a destination resort
thousands of miles away...

1031
01:42:00,531 --> 01:42:03,075
Hiya. Is this the Ludlow affair?

1032
01:42:03,075 --> 01:42:05,036
This is private property.
No, we're on the list, though.

1033
01:42:05,036 --> 01:42:06,746
You have to turn around,
get back in your car.

1034
01:42:06,746 --> 01:42:08,414
This is Dr. Harding,
I'm Dr. Malcolm.

1035
01:42:08,414 --> 01:42:10,708
We have Mr. Ludlow's
test results.

1036
01:42:10,708 --> 01:42:12,418
There's good and bad news.

1037
01:42:12,418 --> 01:42:15,880
Tonight we will christen
Jurassic Park, San Diego,

1038
01:42:15,880 --> 01:42:18,591
with a mega-attraction
that will drive turnstile numbers

1039
01:42:18,591 --> 01:42:21,135
to rival any theme park in the world.

1040
01:42:25,222 --> 01:42:27,725
I want to thank each one of you
for being intrepid enough to turn up

1041
01:42:27,725 --> 01:42:29,894
in these wee small hours
of the morning.

1042
01:42:30,895 --> 01:42:32,813
The ship, it's here.

1043
01:42:32,813 --> 01:42:34,190
It's early?

1044
01:42:34,815 --> 01:42:36,484
You better come now.

1045
01:42:38,361 --> 01:42:39,987
It's so exciting!

1046
01:42:41,989 --> 01:42:44,450
You see that couple?
Sir, you need to look at this.

1047
01:42:44,450 --> 01:42:46,702
Let them see this up close.
Yes, sir.

1048
01:42:46,702 --> 01:42:48,371
Let me check.

1049
01:42:49,163 --> 01:42:52,249
I want a direct line
to the Coast Guard right now.

1050
01:42:52,917 --> 01:42:57,088
Look. That's their transponder signal,
Venture 5888.

1051
01:42:57,088 --> 01:42:59,298
They're headed into port,
but I can't raise them.

1052
01:42:59,298 --> 01:43:01,092
Try again.

1053
01:43:01,092 --> 01:43:04,011
Skipper, SS Venture,
this is InGen Harbor Master.

1054
01:43:04,011 --> 01:43:05,721
Do you copy? Over.

1055
01:43:08,099 --> 01:43:11,852
Skipper, SS Venture, you are approaching
the breakwater at flank speed.

1056
01:43:11,852 --> 01:43:13,437
Reduce at once. Over.

1057
01:43:28,327 --> 01:43:30,830
SS Venture,
reduce your speed at once.

1058
01:43:30,830 --> 01:43:33,290
SS Venture, this is
InGen Harbor Master.

1059
01:43:33,290 --> 01:43:35,418
You are entering a docking area.

1060
01:43:35,418 --> 01:43:37,670
What's everybody looking at?

1061
01:43:42,174 --> 01:43:44,009
We should've stayed in the damn car.

1062
01:44:36,812 --> 01:44:38,314
You okay, Mr. Ludlow?

1063
01:44:38,314 --> 01:44:40,733
I don't know. How do I look?

1064
01:45:12,473 --> 01:45:13,974
Oh, my God.

1065
01:45:13,974 --> 01:45:15,434
Where's the crew?

1066
01:45:16,602 --> 01:45:17,937
All over the place.

1067
01:46:07,611 --> 01:46:09,154
Check the cargo hold!

1068
01:46:09,154 --> 01:46:11,657
There may be crew down there.

1069
01:46:11,657 --> 01:46:13,367
Everybody off the boat!

1070
01:46:21,166 --> 01:46:23,127
No, no! Get away from that!

1071
01:46:56,744 --> 01:46:58,537
Now you're John Hammond.

1072
01:47:28,609 --> 01:47:30,277
Why the hell wasn't it tranquilized?

1073
01:47:30,277 --> 01:47:33,072
It was, Roland hit it with two darts
of concentrated Carfentanil.

1074
01:47:33,072 --> 01:47:35,699
It was over 10 milligrams.
That should've put it into a coma.

1075
01:47:35,699 --> 01:47:38,619
It stopped breathing, so we gave it
Naltrexone to counteract the effects.

1076
01:47:38,619 --> 01:47:39,870
But we didn't know
how much to give it.

1077
01:47:39,870 --> 01:47:42,456
You administered an antagonist
without knowing the proper dosage?

1078
01:47:42,456 --> 01:47:44,083
You put the animal
in a narcoleptic state.

1079
01:47:44,083 --> 01:47:46,335
The thing's a locomotive now.
Look, we're prepared here...

1080
01:47:46,335 --> 01:47:48,587
Are there any more dinosaurs
still on the boat?

1081
01:47:48,587 --> 01:47:50,130
No, we brought the infant back
on the plane.

1082
01:47:50,130 --> 01:47:52,257
We had this to tranquilize it.

1083
01:47:56,887 --> 01:47:58,764
Do you have the infant?

1084
01:48:00,474 --> 01:48:01,976
It's safe.

1085
01:48:04,395 --> 01:48:06,855
Ian. The animal's dehydrated.

1086
01:48:06,855 --> 01:48:08,732
The first it's going to do
is go to a water source

1087
01:48:08,732 --> 01:48:11,735
and then it's going to look for
the next thing its body needs.

1088
01:48:11,735 --> 01:48:14,697
All the containment equipment is here.
We've got to get it back to the dock.

1089
01:48:14,697 --> 01:48:17,449
The boat might still be seaworthy.
I think I know what you're gonna say.

1090
01:48:17,449 --> 01:48:19,743
When we brought the baby
to the trailer, it came.

1091
01:48:19,743 --> 01:48:22,997
There's no reason to think
it won't do the same thing here.

1092
01:48:25,082 --> 01:48:26,500
Yep.

1093
01:48:28,419 --> 01:48:30,087
Where's the infant?

1094
01:48:33,007 --> 01:48:35,467
It's in a secured facility.
Why?

1095
01:48:38,512 --> 01:48:40,305
Where's the facility?

1096
01:50:04,973 --> 01:50:07,226
There's a dinosaur in our backyard.

1097
01:50:13,649 --> 01:50:15,901
Benjamin, what are you doing?

1098
01:50:15,901 --> 01:50:17,486
What are you doing?

1099
01:50:18,195 --> 01:50:20,322
What? What?

1100
01:50:20,322 --> 01:50:23,033
Come on. There's a dinosaur
in our backyard.

1101
01:50:23,033 --> 01:50:25,619
The problem is,
you need to read to him.

1102
01:50:25,619 --> 01:50:28,288
No, no, no.
I will tell you exactly what it is.

1103
01:50:28,288 --> 01:50:30,457
You have to serve him dinner,
he won't be up all night.

1104
01:50:30,457 --> 01:50:32,251
It's the stupid fish tank!
It is not the fish tank!

1105
01:50:32,251 --> 01:50:35,170
He needs a proper nightlight.
Then put something over it!

1106
01:50:35,170 --> 01:50:37,172
It may as well be daytime.
It isn't.

1107
01:50:37,172 --> 01:50:38,882
Just turn it off and...

1108
01:51:00,154 --> 01:51:02,239
Slow down. Slow it down!

1109
01:51:26,346 --> 01:51:27,848
There it is.

1110
01:51:33,979 --> 01:51:35,647
He's sedated.

1111
01:51:37,774 --> 01:51:39,902
Really heavily.

1112
01:51:39,902 --> 01:51:41,653
Give me a hand.

1113
01:51:44,573 --> 01:51:47,951
When the adult sees us
once again with his baby,

1114
01:51:47,951 --> 01:51:50,078
isn't he going to be like... You!

1115
01:51:51,079 --> 01:51:54,958
There may be some angry recognition.

1116
01:51:56,335 --> 01:51:59,338
Who knows?
He may be just happy to see us.

1117
01:51:59,338 --> 01:52:01,924
Hey! What the hell
do you think you're you doing?

1118
01:52:03,467 --> 01:52:05,177
We're taking the kid.

1119
01:52:05,802 --> 01:52:08,847
If you really want to stop us,
shoot us.

1120
01:52:10,641 --> 01:52:12,726
How will we find the adult?

1121
01:52:12,726 --> 01:52:13,936
Follow the screams.

1122
01:52:40,254 --> 01:52:42,506
Get to the other side of the bus!

1123
01:53:34,308 --> 01:53:36,476
There.
Oh, my God.

1124
01:53:50,657 --> 01:53:52,534
It's too drugged.
He's never going to know we have it

1125
01:53:52,534 --> 01:53:54,161
if the thing won't make
some kind of sound!

1126
01:53:54,161 --> 01:53:55,996
Come on. Wake up. Come on.

1127
01:53:55,996 --> 01:53:58,081
Come on. Wake up.

1128
01:54:04,588 --> 01:54:06,048
He knows.

1129
01:54:21,730 --> 01:54:23,857
Ian, slow down a little.

1130
01:54:25,484 --> 01:54:27,194
I don't think so.

1131
01:54:47,297 --> 01:54:49,383
This guy's almost fully awake.

1132
01:54:49,383 --> 01:54:50,884
You know where you're going?

1133
01:54:50,884 --> 01:54:54,554
Yeah, the waterfront's on
the other side of these warehouses.

1134
01:54:54,554 --> 01:54:56,848
Is there a way through?

1135
01:54:56,848 --> 01:54:58,809
God. There could be.

1136
01:55:04,231 --> 01:55:05,732
There we go.

1137
01:55:08,318 --> 01:55:10,278
You have to follow me now.

1138
01:55:10,278 --> 01:55:11,571
There we go.

1139
01:55:12,155 --> 01:55:14,408
Got him?
Yep.

1140
01:55:14,408 --> 01:55:16,284
Here we go. Ready?

1141
01:55:16,284 --> 01:55:19,204
Where's the Rex?
Is it still behind us?

1142
01:55:27,003 --> 01:55:28,130
There's the water.

1143
01:55:28,130 --> 01:55:30,966
Shoot it. Shoot it.
Tell them to shoot it.

1144
01:55:35,262 --> 01:55:38,348
No, you idiot, the adult.
Shoot the adult.

1145
01:55:38,348 --> 01:55:40,475
I want the baby back alive.

1146
01:56:09,963 --> 01:56:11,256
Dr. Malcolm!

1147
01:56:13,091 --> 01:56:15,802
What have you done with it?
I want that infant!

1148
01:56:47,292 --> 01:56:48,835
Are you there?

1149
01:57:19,324 --> 01:57:21,368
There you are.

1150
01:57:41,054 --> 01:57:42,764
Wait. Wait.

1151
01:59:51,601 --> 01:59:55,271
There's a really first-rate shot
of the deck of this ship,

1152
01:59:55,271 --> 01:59:58,108
and the cargo hold that,
for the moment anyway,

1153
01:59:58,108 --> 02:00:02,487
contains the animal itself,
presumably with the infant alongside.

1154
02:00:02,487 --> 02:00:07,534
By our calculations, they should be
nearing the halfway point of this trip.

1155
02:00:07,534 --> 02:00:09,619
Jim, can you still hear me there?

1156
02:00:10,286 --> 02:00:13,331
Yes, I can, Bernard.
We are in fact halfway to the island.

1157
02:00:13,331 --> 02:00:17,085
It is 206 nautical miles
from our present location.

1158
02:00:17,085 --> 02:00:18,837
The ship is moving at about 20 knots

1159
02:00:18,837 --> 02:00:22,215
which will put it in at about
11:30 a.m., Eastern time.

1160
02:00:22,215 --> 02:00:25,885
One of the Navy's primary concerns
throughout all of this has been safety.

1161
02:00:25,885 --> 02:00:29,389
And if we take another look around
the ever-growing escort around the ship

1162
02:00:29,389 --> 02:00:33,685
they're taking no chances of a repeat
of the San Diego incident.

1163
02:00:33,685 --> 02:00:36,062
Okay. We're going to take
a moment here

1164
02:00:36,062 --> 02:00:40,108
and run the tape of our interview
earlier today with John Hammond.

1165
02:00:40,108 --> 02:00:43,278
He's the former head of
InGen Bio-Engineering,

1166
02:00:43,278 --> 02:00:46,072
the man who has come forward
to spearhead this movement

1167
02:00:46,072 --> 02:00:48,575
not only to return these animals
to their island

1168
02:00:48,575 --> 02:00:51,786
but to keep the island itself intact.

1169
02:00:52,537 --> 02:00:56,249
It is absolutely imperative

1170
02:00:56,249 --> 02:01:00,503
that we work with the Costa Rican
Department of Biological Preserves

1171
02:01:00,503 --> 02:01:02,756
to establish a set of rules

1172
02:01:03,381 --> 02:01:07,844
for the preservation
and isolation of that island.

1173
02:01:08,636 --> 02:01:13,975
These creatures require
our absence to survive,

1174
02:01:13,975 --> 02:01:15,643
not our help.

1175
02:01:15,643 --> 02:01:19,230
And if we could only step aside...

1176
02:01:20,690 --> 02:01:22,650
and trust in nature...

1177
02:01:23,651 --> 02:01:26,154
life will find a way.